    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d218">

                <pb facs="0718" n="696" sameAs="frus:frus1946v02/pg696"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Council/6–2946: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the United States Delegation at the
                            Council of Foreign Ministers in Paris</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-06-29T17:00:00-04:00">June 29, 1946—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3181. Secdel 383. Ital Amb yesterday presented
                        note<anchor n="98" xml:id="d218-f1"/> re alleged Ital negotiations with USSR
                    on reparations and commercial relations. Note recalls that Ital Govt had given
                        Dept last Sept and subsequently <pb
                        facs="0719" n="697" xml:id="pg697"/> full info on these matters, including
                    Soviet suggestion re construction of ships for USSR in Ital yards, and states
                    “in most emphatic and clear manner” that US, UK und French concern or suspicion
                    is not warranted by any action taken by Ital Govt unknown to Allies.</p>
                <p>Tarchiani stressed that initiative in several conversations on these subjects
                    between Ital and USSR reps had been on initiative of latter and that while Itals
                    were in no position to rebuff Soviets, commitments were avoided and US kept
                    informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[On June 29, 1946, Vasil Kolarov, President of the Bulgarian National
                        Assembly, and a leader in the Bulgarian Communist Party, called upon the
                        Secretary of State. In the course of the conversation, Kolarov set forth his
                        views on the favorable treatment that Bulgaria should receive in the peace
                        treaty. A summary of the conversation was transmitted in telegram 3239, July
                        1, from Paris, printed in <ref target="frus:frus1946v06/pg_110">Volume VI,
                            page 110</ref>.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
